    &lt;summary&gt;Selected Publications (click to expand)&lt;/summary&gt;
        &lt;!-- ===== Interacting with AI (2023-now) ===== --&gt;
        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="flipflop" data-title="FlipFlop Experiment" data-venue="arXiv"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.08596"
            data-root-node="1" data-root-name="Interacting with AI|2023-now" data-root-color="#D1BDFF"
            data-coauthors="Lidiya Murakhovs'ka, Caiming Xiong, Chien-Sheng Wu"
            data-full-title="Are You Sure? Challenging LLMs Leads to Performance Drops in The FlipFlop Experiment"
            data-summary="What happens when you give an LLM a classification task, it gets it right, and you ask it 'Are you sure?'. Universally, LLMs tend to flip their answer: favoring to a superficial agreement with the user over being accurate. We connect the FlipFlop effect with the connect of sycophancy in LLMs, and show that finetuning is not a panacea to cure the issue, although it helps a bit."&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="lost_in_conv" data-title="Lost in Conversation" data-venue="ICLR2026"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.06120" data-parent="flipflop"
            data-coauthors="Hiroaki Hayashi, Yingbo Zhou, Jennifer Neville"
            data-full-title="LLMs Get Lost In Multi-Turn Conversation"
            data-summary="A large-scale simulation experiment with 15 LLMs. We show that on the exact same set of tasks, model performance degrades (39%!) in multi-turn underspecified conversation, compared to single-turn, fully-specified conversations. We find that this is because when models take a wrong turn, they get lost and do not recover (reliability issue rather than aptitude)."
            data-links-code="https://github.com/microsoft/lost_in_conversation"
            data-links-data="https://huggingface.co/datasets/microsoft/lost_in_conversation"&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="delegate52" data-title="DELEGATE52" data-venue="arxiv"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.15597" data-parent="lost_in_conv" data-latest="1"
            data-coauthors="Tobias Schnabel, Jennifer Neville"
            data-full-title="LLMs Corrupt Your Documents When You Delegate"
            data-summary="We create work environments in 52 professional domains (such as crystallography, aviation, weaving, Python programming) and simulate long-horizon delegated workstreams to study current AI system readiness for delegated workflows (i.e., vibe-coding). We find that current LLMs corrupt documents during delegated workflows, and are not yet ready for delegation across diverse professional contexts."
            data-links-code="https://github.com/microsoft/delegate52"
            data-links-data="https://huggingface.co/datasets/microsoft/delegate52"&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="userlm" data-title="UserLM" data-venue="ICLR2026"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06552" data-parent="lost_in_conv"
            data-coauthors="Tarek Naous, Wei Xu, Jennifer Neville"
            data-full-title="Flipping the Dialogue: Training and Evaluating User Language Models"
            data-summary="We train an 8b LLM specifically to simulate the user role in user-AI conversations. We find that this model is much more diverse in natural in simulating users than prompting assistant models (the standard approach). We argue for the development of better user-simulating models, as we show assistant models are limited in their simulation capabilities."
            data-links-model="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/UserLM-8b"&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;!-- ===== Writing/Editing (2020-now) ===== --&gt;
        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="keepitsimple" data-title="Keep It Simple" data-venue="ACL2021"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03444"
            data-root-node="1" data-root-name="Writing/Editing|2020-now" data-root-color="#B3F5BC"
            data-coauthors="Tobias Schnabel, Paul Bennett, Marti A. Hearst"
            data-full-title="Keep it Simple: Unsupervised Simplification of Multi-Paragraph Text"
            data-summary="First baby steps in the field of simplification (a sister task to summarization). We approached it in an entirely unsupervised way (a la Summary Loop), by crafting rewards for fluency, relevance, and simplicity, and optimizing through RL. Neat Finding: an algorithmic improvement to the popular SCST algorithm (k-SCST) which yielded much more stable training, and better models. Also: a focus on paragraph-level (rather than sentence-level) simplification, which was not so common at the time!"
            data-links-code="https://github.com/tingofurro/keep_it_simple"
            data-links-video="https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.498.mp4"
            data-links-model="https://huggingface.co/philippelaban/keep_it_simple"&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="swipe" data-title="SWiPE" data-venue="ACL2023"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19204" data-parent="keepitsimple"
            data-coauthors="Jesse Vig, Wojciech Kryscinski, Caiming Xiong, Chien-Sheng Wu"
            data-full-title="SWiPE: A Dataset for Document-Level Simplification of Wikipedia Pages"
            data-summary="Improved the extraction process of simplification document pairs from Simple Wikipedia, showing that Swiki is a great resource to study the simplification process. Prior work had mostly disregarded SWiki in favor of less publicly available datasets such as Newsela."
            data-links-code="https://github.com/salesforce/simplification"&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="art_or_artifice" data-title="Art or Artifice" data-venue="CHI2024"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14556" data-parent="swipe"
            data-coauthors="Tuhin Chakrabarty, Chien-Sheng Wu"
            data-full-title="Art or Artifice? Large Language Models and the False Promise of Creativity"
            data-summary="Work led by Tuhin Chakrabarty as an intern at Salesforce Research. Are LLMs capable of high-quality creative fictional writing, to the level of short-stories published in the New Yorker. We designed a methodology to evaluate story quality (through rigorous manual evaluation by expert writers), and found a very large gap between professionally-written and AI-generated stories. Neat Finding: at the time, LLMs were not capable of even judging which stories were better than others, and tended to favor more bland AI-like writing."
            data-links-code="https://github.com/salesforce/creativity_eval"
            data-links-data_hf="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Salesforce/ttcw_creativity_eval"
            data-links-video="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3613904.3642731"&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;article class="paper-entry" data-id="ai_writing_idiosyncracies" data-title="Writing Idiosyncracies" data-venue="CHI2025"
            data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.14509" data-parent="art_or_artifice" data-is-left-child="1"
            data-coauthors="Tuhin Chakrabarty, Chien-Sheng Wu"
            data-full-title="Can AI writing be salvaged? Mitigating Idiosyncrasies and Improving Human-AI Alignment in the Writing Process through Edits"
            data-summary="Work led by Tuhin at Salesforce Research. Following up on Art or Artifice. Lowering our expectation from a story (1000+ words) to a single paragraph (100-200 words). We find in a controlled experiment that LLMs still struggle to generate high-quality creative writing even in shorter form. Neat finding: when allowing an LLM to: (1) draft, (2) detect issues, (3) edit them through executable edits, then LLMs are capable of i
