    <text>Your garden has 23 papers on 4 branches. Since your last update (DELEGATE52 and UserLM, in April), Scholar lists about a dozen new non-patent papers. Here's what I'd add and where, split into strong picks and optional ones.

## Strong picks (6)

**Interacting with AI branch.** This is where most of the new work is.

| Paper | Venue | Attach to | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| **LLMs Get Lost in Evolving User Intent** (Tack, Laban, Neville) | arXiv 2607.20734 | child of `lost_in_conv` | A direct sequel to Lost in Conversation, and it already has 11 citations |
| **Babysitting AI Agents** (Mittal, Kim, Counts, Neville, Das, Laban) | arXiv 2610.34748 | child of `delegate52` | You're senior author, and it's a natural follow-on to the delegation work |
| **Real vs. Simulated User Behaviors** (Mehri, Laban, ... Levine, Galley) | arXiv 2605.07847 | child of `userlm` | The strongest of the user-simulation follow-ups, with 11 citations |

**Summarization branch.** Factuality and attribution are still growing here.

| Paper | Venue | Attach to | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| **DeepTRACE** (Venkit, Laban, ... Wu) | ICLR 2026 | child of `answer_engine_eval` | A direct follow-up (from answer engines to deep research), published at a main venue |
| **Attribution Gradients** (Kambhamettu, Hwang, Laban, Head) | UIST 2026 | child of `answer_engine_eval`, indirect link to `minicheck` | Adds an HCI angle to attribution, and links nicely to InkSync |

## Optional: pick at most one

The user-simulation cluster is busy. Under UserLM there are also *Stay On Task and Sound Natural* (Gul et al.), *Learning User Representations* (Mehri et al.) and *KNOWSIM* (EMNLP 2026). Adding all of them would crowd that part of the garden. I'd add **Stay On Task** as a second child of `userlm` if you want one more, and skip the other two.

## I'd leave these out

- **Agentic Context Management** (you're 5th author), plus *KRISTEVA*, *Visual Arithmetic*, *BingoGuard*, *CRMArena*, *EvalAgent*, *Creative Planning* and *Prompt Leakage*. These are collaborations that sit outside your main lines of work.
- **RAG Sub-question Coverage** and **SummExecEdit** could go under the Summary of a Haystack and SummEdits nodes, but they're minor, so I'd skip them to keep the garden lean.
- The workshop and tutorial papers, and all the patents.
- The **Writing/Editing** branch gets nothing new. Your newer work is mostly on interaction and delegation.

## Housekeeping

- **Latest marker:** `data-latest` is on DELEGATE52. I'd move it to Babysitting AI Agents or Evolving User Intent, whichever you want highlighted.
- **Answer Engine title:** the FAccT 2025 version on Scholar has a different title from the one in the garden ("…a qualitative understanding to the false promise…"). You may want to switch to the published title.
- **AI-Slop to AI-Polish:** Scholar still lists it as arXiv. If it has since been accepted somewhere, update the venue.
- **Scholar duplicates:** your profile lists *Lost in Conversation* twice, and *Search Engines in an AI Era* and *Prompt Leakage* also have duplicate entries. Merging them on Scholar would combine the citation counts.

If you tell me which ones to add, I'll write the `&lt;article&gt;` entries in your summary style ("Neat Finding: …"), drafted from each paper's abstract for you to edit. I'll also move the latest marker and check that the garden renders.</text>
