          <text>Report on 4 papers. Three arXiv abstract pages were read; paper 4 was not found.

Reporting note: the arXiv pages are from a non-preapproved source, so I could not reproduce the full abstracts verbatim. Below each abstract is a faithful paraphrase. The full text is at the abs URL.

1. LLMs Get Lost in Evolving User Intent
- Title: "LLMs Get Lost in Evolving User Intent"
- Authors: Jihoon Tack, Philippe Laban, Jennifer Neville
- arXiv abs URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.20734 (v1, submitted 22 Jul 2026; cs.LG; 20 pages, 10 figures)
- Abstract (paraphrased): LLMs are increasingly used as collaborative agents, but users reveal and revise their intent over a conversation. Models are still mostly evaluated and trained in single-turn, fully specified settings. The authors propose a framework that turns static single-turn tasks into multi-turn conversations where the user's intent evolves (revealed incrementally, revised, sometimes redirected). It keeps each task's original evaluation protocol, so existing benchmarks can be reused without new annotation. Across tasks, strong static performance does not carry over to the evolving-intent setting, with large drops across model families. The authors conclude that current LLMs do not yet reliably track and act on evolving user intent.
- Code/data/model/demo links on the abstract page: none. The page has PDF, HTML (experimental), and TeX source links, plus CC BY 4.0 license. The "Code, Data and Media" and "Demos" sections are generic toggles with no specific project links.

2. DeepTRACE
- Title: "DeepTRACE: Auditing Deep Research AI Systems for Tracking Reliability Across Citations and Evidence"
- Authors: Pranav Narayanan Venkit, Philippe Laban, Yilun Zhou, Kung-Hsiang Huang, Yixin Mao, Chien-Sheng Wu
- arXiv abs URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04499 (v1, submitted 2 Sep 2025; cs.CL, cs.AI)
- Abstract (paraphrased): Generative search engines and deep research agents promise source-grounded answers, but they show overconfidence, weak sourcing, and confusing citation practices. The authors introduce DeepTRACE, an audit framework that turns community-identified failure cases into eight measurable dimensions covering answer text, sources, and citations. It uses statement-level analysis (decomposition and confidence scoring) and builds citation and factual-support matrices. They evaluate public systems including GPT-4.5/5, You.com, Perplexity, Copilot/Bing, and Gemini, using an LLM judge with validated agreement with human raters, across web-search and deep-research configurations. Findings: these systems often give one-sided, highly confident answers on debate queries and include many statements unsupported by their own cited sources. Deep-research configurations reduce overconfidence and reach high citation thoroughness but remain one-sided on debate queries and still have many unsupported statements. Citation accuracy ranges from 40% to 80% across systems.
- Code/data/model/demo links on the abstract page: none. The abstract mentions "You.com" as a link text to http://You.com, which is a system name, not a project link. Page has PDF, HTML, TeX source. Comment: "arXiv admin note: text overlap with arXiv:2410.22349".

3. Towards User Simulators That Stay On Task And Still Sound Natural
- Title: "Towards User Simulators That Stay On Task And Still Sound Natural"
- Authors: Mustafa Omer Gul, Philippe Laban, Tanya Goyal, Jennifer Neville
- arXiv abs URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31512 (v1, submitted 22 Oct 2026; cs.CL; 30 pages, 16 figures, 6 tables; "In submission")
- Abstract (paraphrased): Users rely on AI systems for long multi-turn tasks, and performance degrades over long interactions, so user simulators must follow complex intents while sounding realistic. Prompted assistants follow complex intents but lack behavioral fidelity. Specialized simulators sound more natural but drift from complex intents. The authors propose a reinforcement learning recipe that jointly rewards task-intent adherence and behavioral naturalness. Naturalness is operationalized with a lightweight discriminator trained in an alternating adversarial loop with the simulator. They release the resulting model, UserLM-2.0, which produces valid simulated conversations (meeting both task-faithfulness and naturalness) 46.2% of the time, versus 14.1% for the strongest prior user simulator.
- Code/data/model/demo links on the abstract page: none with a URL. The abstract says the model "UserLM-2.0" is released, but the page gives no link to it. Page has PDF, HTML, TeX source, and a CC BY 4.0 license link.

