Links: [{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.00361v1"},{"title":"[2510.00361] Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00361"},{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.00361"},{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers - ADS","url":"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025arXiv251000361K/abstract"},{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers | Read Paper on Bytez","url":"https://bytez.com/docs/arxiv/2510.00361/paper"},{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers — Recommender Systems","url":"https://awesomepapers.io/recommender-systems/papers/2510.00361"},{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers — Graph Learning","url":"https://awesomepapers.io/graph-learning/papers/2510.00361"},{"title":"Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers · Pith","url":"https://pith.science/paper/2510.00361"},{"title":"Papers | Semantic Scholar","url":"https://semanticscholar.org/paper/Attribution-Gradients:-Incrementally-Unfolding-for-Kambhamettu-Hwang/d1bfc8ed43ab916b4221f255310394b28be1fff3"},{"title":"Penn and Microsoft Turn AI Citations Into Layered Evidence Trails | AlphaSignal","url":"https://alphasignal.ai/news/penn-and-microsoft-turn-ai-citations-into-layered-evidence-trails"}]

The paper is "Attribution Gradients: Incrementally Unfolding Citations for Critical Examination of Attributed AI Answers" (arXiv 2510.00361), by Hita Kambhamettu, Alyssa Hwang, Philippe Laban, and Andrew Head. It proposes a citation interface for AI answers.

- **Problem:** The authors note that reading the cited sources is costly, and citation links themselves offer little guidance about what evidence they contain.
- **Approach:** The interface gathers evidence counts, supporting and contradictory excerpts, source links, and explanations in one place, and lets readers unfold second-degree citations in place. A user can break an answer sentence into its claims, and color-coded clusters of evidence show direct support or contradiction.
- **Audit finding:** In a case study of the OpenSciLM QA system, the authors found that the citations in OpenSciLM often failed to support claims in a generated answer.
- **User study:** The lab study reported that participants produced substantially higher-quality revisions of AI-generated claims. A secondary summary gives a 15-person study and a rise in evidence recall from 17.3% to 51.3%, but that figure comes from a news write-up, not the paper itself.
- **Limits:** The authors say evidence comes only from scientific QA with readers experienced in academic literature. The prototype is a research system with no public code.

The arXiv listing shows the paper is recent, and the authors describe it as a v2 submission from 2025-10-01.


